  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the sum of all integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which the number \\( 97_b \\) (interpreted as a base \\( b \\) number) is divisible by \\( 17_b \\) (also interpreted as a base \\( b \\) number). Here's how we can approach it:\n\n### Step 1: Convert the Base \\( b \\) Numbers to Base 10\n\nFirst, we convert both \\( 97_b \\) and \\( 17_b \\) to their base 10 equivalents.\n\n- \\( 97_b = 9 \\cdot b + 7 \\)\n- \\( 17_b = 1 \\cdot b + 7 \\)\n\n### Step 2: Set Up the Divisibility Condition\n\nWe need \\( 97_b \\) to be divisible by \\( 17_b \\). In base 10, this translates to:\n\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Simplify the Congruence\n\nWe can simplify the congruence as follows:\n\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( 9b \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nb + 7 \\mid 7\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Find Possible Values of \\( b \\)\n\nSince \\( b + 7 \\) must be a divisor of 7, and \\( b > 9 \\), we consider the positive divisors of 7:\n\n\\[\n7 = 7 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 7 = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( b + 7 = 7 \\), which implies:\n\n\\[\nb = 0\n\\]\n\nHowever, \\( b = 0 \\) is not a valid base for a number system. Therefore, there are no valid bases \\( b > 9 \\) that satisfy the condition.\n\n### Step 5: Conclusion\n\nSince there are no valid bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which \\( 97_b \\) is divisible by \\( 17_b \\), the sum of such bases is 0.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
